WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:06.900
A career worst debut, solving a murder on&nbsp;
vacation and saving Halloween. Here's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:10.129
ranking of Adam Sandler's Netflix original movies.

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:16.380
Adam Sandler's Netflix tenure got off to&nbsp;
a rocky start with 2015's "The Ridiculous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:21.840
6," the worst reviewed film of his career.&nbsp;
Taking its inspiration from "The Magnificent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:27.480
Seven," this comedic Western casts Sandler&nbsp;
as Tommy "White Knife" Dunson Stockburn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:31.440
a white man raised by Native&nbsp;
Americans. Much to his surprise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:37.560
Tommy learns he has five outlaw half-brothers:&nbsp;
happy-go-lucky roughneck "Lil Pete" Stockburn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:43.980
Mexican burro rider Ramon Lopez Stockburn,&nbsp;
saloon pianist Chico Stockburn, mountain man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:48.840
Herm Stockburn,, and former bodyguard&nbsp;
to Abraham Lincoln Danny Stockburn.

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:54.660
Together, this merry band of misfits set out&nbsp;
to save their father, Frank, a dying bank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.660 --> 00:01:01.200
robber who's been kidnapped by bandits looking for&nbsp;
$50,000 in loot he's set aside for Tommy's tribe.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:06.180
According to The Guardian, the production&nbsp;
was plagued with controversy, as about a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:10.500
dozen Native American actors walked off&nbsp;
the set in protest of its depiction of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:16.140
Apache culture. Their complaints ranged from&nbsp;
characters named Beaver Breath and Never Wears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:21.300
Bra to a scene involving a Native American&nbsp;
woman smoking a peace pipe while squatting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:25.320
and urinating, as well as the improper&nbsp;
positioning of feathers on a teepee.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:28.332
"I had dream about you last night White Knife."

00:01:28.332 --> 00:01:30.012
"Oh, that's nice."

00:01:30.012 --> 00:01:33.660
"Not nice dream. In dream you naughty."

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:38.820
Perhaps its most positive review came from&nbsp;
Michael Phillips of The Chicago Tribune,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:42.480
who said it was "a long, long way&nbsp;
from the worst Sandler movie ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:45.951
made." But that was about as&nbsp;
kind as it got for the film.

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:51.360
The second Adam Sandler Netflix original was&nbsp;
only marginally better received than the first:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:56.100
whereas "The Ridiculous 6" clocked in&nbsp;
with an abysmal 0% Rotten Tomatoes score,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.100 --> 00:02:02.280
2016's "The Do-Over" earned a 9% rating from&nbsp;
the critical aggregate. So things were already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:05.400
on the upswing for the Sandman's&nbsp;
output at the streaming service.

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:10.320
The film reunites Sandler with his old&nbsp;
"Saturday Night Live" buddy David Spade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:15.900
who plays Charlie, a hapless bank manager living&nbsp;
in Florida with his wife and stepkids. While his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:19.800
wife is cheating on him with her ex-husband,&nbsp;
Charlie reunites with his high school friend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:24.360
Max, played by Sandler, an FBI agent&nbsp;
who invites him to party on his rented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:29.280
yacht. Max blows up the vessel and fakes their&nbsp;
deaths, allowing them to assume new identities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:32.640
but their chance for a do-over is&nbsp;
met with comedic complications.

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:35.580
The vast majority of critics weren't as forgiving,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:39.390
with David Ehrlich of Indiewire&nbsp;
summing it up by writing -

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:43.740
"'The Do-Over' is atrocious, but it's&nbsp;
atrocious in different ways than any of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:46.200
Adam Sandler's previous comedies. [...] For years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:50.460
we've been asking Adam Sandler to try&nbsp;
harder. We've been making a huge mistake."

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:57.180
Although the majority of its reviews were largely&nbsp;
negative, many critics lauded 2018's "The Week&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:01.500
Of" as being more personal than some of&nbsp;
Adam Sandler's other Netflix offerings.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:07.260
Sandler plays Kenny Lustig, a working class Long&nbsp;
Islander struggling to pay for his daughter's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:12.180
wedding. Kenny cuts every corner possible to&nbsp;
avoid asking the wealthy father of the groom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:17.580
Kirby, for help, booking the venue at a&nbsp;
cheap hotel and hiring an adolescent DJ.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:22.260
Things get out of hand when Kirby shows up&nbsp;
and immediately starts feuding with Kenny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:27.600
over everything. But before long the two dads&nbsp;
are bonding over the shared loss of their kids.

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:33.180
Jesse Hassenger of The AV Club credited&nbsp;
longtime Sandler friend and first-time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:39.180
director Robert Smigel, who voices Triumph the&nbsp;
Insult Comic Dog and co-wrote Sandler's "You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:43.380
Don't Mess with the Zohan," with elevating&nbsp;
the film above the standard Sandler fare.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:48.540
Yet his opinion was in the minority. Writing&nbsp;
for The New York Times, Glenn Kenny said -

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:51.660
"While the movie is ultimately&nbsp;
more of the same old same old,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:57.240
it is at least not as appallingly sexist and&nbsp;
culturally insensitive as 'The Ridiculous Six.'"

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:03.000
"Sandy Wexler" provides Adam Sandler with&nbsp;
the opportunity to indulge in one of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:08.280
favorite cinematic pastimes: transforming into&nbsp;
a wacky character akin to the kinds he used to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:13.140
play on "Saturday Night Live." While those&nbsp;
"SNL" performances were only sketch length,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:16.980
this 2017 film is stretched out to 131 minutes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:21.480
testing the patience of many critics who&nbsp;
aren't always on the Sandman's wavelength.

00:04:24.355 --> 00:04:25.800
"That's great."

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:32.220
Wexler is a middling talent agent in 1990s Los&nbsp;
Angeles, characterized by his nasally voice and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:37.380
giant spectacles. He's devoted to his clients,&nbsp;
all of whom are hanging on the fringes of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:43.200
industry, from ventriloquists to wrestlers to&nbsp;
wannabe movie stars. Sandy meets his newest find,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:47.760
the talented singer Courtney, while she's&nbsp;
performing at Six Flags, and he instantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:52.860
signs her. But Sandy's devotion to his work is put&nbsp;
to the test when he falls in love with Courtney.

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:58.380
Although Brian Tallerico of RogerEbert.com&nbsp;
thought Sandler's third Netflix title was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:01.740
better than "The Ridiculous 6" or&nbsp;
"The Do-Over," he also pointed out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:05.340
that it was more of a case for how&nbsp;
bad the first two films were. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:09.840
he admitted that the film had its positive&nbsp;
aspects, including Sandler's performance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:15.000
which he likened to his work in "Punch-Drunk&nbsp;
Love" and "Funny People." Tallerico added -

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:20.640
"There's a halfway decent and interesting&nbsp;
90-minute dramedy buried in this 131-minute film."

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:24.540
Jesse Hassenger of The AV Club&nbsp;
was more positive, writing -

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:28.860
"This is all still Sandler schtick,&nbsp;
replete with endless running gags,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:32.536
but it's also surprisingly&nbsp;
endearing and often quite funny."

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:38.520
"Murder Mystery" was among the most popular&nbsp;
Netflix originals ever made, bringing in 83&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:44.580
million viewers and inspiring an upcoming sequel.&nbsp;
The 2019 film was one of Adam Sandler's best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:50.100
reviewed outings on the streamer, and one of the&nbsp;
best of his vacation movies AKA movies the Sandman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:55.320
made as an excuse to visit an exotic locale with&nbsp;
his friends and make a few million while there.

00:05:55.320 --> 00:06:01.200
Sandler plays Nick Spitz, a New York City police&nbsp;
officer taking a long-delayed European vacation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:06.780
with his wife, hairdresser Audrey, played by&nbsp;
Jennifer Aniston. While on the plane they meet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:11.520
dashing aristocrat Charles Cavendish, who&nbsp;
invites them to party on the luxury yacht&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:17.280
owned by his wealthy uncle, Malcolm Quince.&nbsp;
Quince, who is engaged to Charles' ex-fiancee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:22.260
is found murdered by his own dagger, and&nbsp;
Nick and Audrey are framed for the crime.

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:26.700
Although he had issues with Sandler's&nbsp;
character, Richard Lawson of Vanity Fair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:32.040
called the film "a cheery enough diversion."&nbsp;
Leah Greenblatt of Entertainment Weekly wrote,

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:36.480
"Aniston and Sandler [...] relax into&nbsp;
their roles as if their only stake in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:41.392
Mystery is to enjoy the free trip to Italy&nbsp;
and have fun running down cobblestones."

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:45.900
Critically speaking, Adam Sandler's&nbsp;
Netflix output has only gotten better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:50.220
with each subsequent release. One of&nbsp;
his latest, 2020's "Hubie Halloween,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:54.900
got decent reviews relative to how the&nbsp;
Sandman's movies are usually received.

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:59.700
Hubie Dubois is one of Sandler's signature&nbsp;
adult man-children, in line with those he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:04.380
played in "Billy Madison" and "The Waterboy."&nbsp;
He's a sweet-natured simpleton who's constantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:09.180
ridiculed by his neighbors and the local&nbsp;
sheriff, Sgt. Steve Downey. As the official&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:13.920
Halloween Helper of Salem, Massachusetts, he&nbsp;
keeps an eye out for teenagers attempting pranks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:18.840
only to find himself the butt of many cruel&nbsp;
jokes from kids and adults alike. But things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:24.900
get really spooky on October 31st, as escaped&nbsp;
lunatic Richie Hartman and possible werewolf&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:29.400
Walter Lambert wreak havoc on the town.&nbsp;
In the end, Hubie comes to the rescue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:34.320
and along the way he finds romance with&nbsp;
Sgt. Downey's ex-wife, Violet Valentine.

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:38.760
Released at the height of the COVID-19&nbsp;
pandemic, "Hubie Halloween" proved to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:42.420
be the perfect October surprise for&nbsp;
viewers stuck in their living rooms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:46.860
holding the top spot on Netflix for 10&nbsp;
days straight. Critics were also generally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:51.570
surprised as well with the movie, with John&nbsp;
DeFore of The Hollywood Reporter writing -

00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:54.780
"Those choosing to stay at home instead&nbsp;
of trick-or-treating during this year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:58.131
of unfunny horrors could do much worse than this."

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:03.240
Whether it's attending Lakers games with his&nbsp;
"Anger Management" costar Jack Nicholson or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:06.540
gaining the admiration of Shaquille&nbsp;
O'Neal for his prowess with the ball,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:11.280
Adam Sandler has long had an affinity for&nbsp;
basketball. He even played a gambling addict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:16.260
who bets it all on a crucial NBA match&nbsp;
in "Uncut Gems." His love of the game is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:21.300
evident in every frame of 2022's "Hustle,"&nbsp;
one of his best-reviewed Netflix originals.

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:27.180
Sandler plays Stanley Sugerman, a world-weary&nbsp;
talent scout for the Philadelphia 76ers whose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:31.680
search for the perfect basketball player has&nbsp;
taken a toll on his personal life. While in Spain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:36.540
he meets Bo Cruz, whose troubled past has kept&nbsp;
him from playing professionally. Impressed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.540 --> 00:08:39.720
with his streetballing skills,&nbsp;
Stanley brings Bo to the U.S.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:43.140
and the two men find redemption&nbsp;
through their shared love of the game.

00:08:43.473 --> 00:08:45.693
"Do you want to be in the NBA?"

00:08:45.693 --> 00:08:46.673
"Yes!"

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:49.380
"Well let's make that happen."

00:08:49.654 --> 00:08:54.420
Critics praised the film's authenticity of&nbsp;
its basketball world, as well as Sandler's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:58.140
performance. The actor has at times&nbsp;
been accused of phoning in performances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.140 --> 00:09:03.600
but "Hustle" provided a breath of fresh air&nbsp;
for critics, with Peter Travers of ABC saying -

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:07.200
"Sandler again shows what he can&nbsp;
do when fully committed to a role."

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:12.360
Whether it's in his signature outrageous&nbsp;
comedies or more auteur-driven dramas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:16.440
Adam Sandler has cornered the market&nbsp;
on playing adult men stuck in a state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:21.300
of arrested development. Much like Paul&nbsp;
Thomas Anderson's "Punch-Drunk Love," Noah&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:26.280
Baumbach's "The Meyerowitz Stories" burrows&nbsp;
beneath Sandler's onscreen persona to examine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:31.080
the deep emotional damage at its core. This&nbsp;
provided him with one of his very best roles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:35.700
and perhaps his best Netflix original,&nbsp;
albeit one not produced by Happy Madison.

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:41.100
He plays Danny Meyerowitz, a talented musician&nbsp;
stuck under the shadow of his domineering father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.100 --> 00:09:46.560
sculptor Harold Meyerowitz. Danny moves in&nbsp;
with his dad and his newest wife, alcoholic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:51.720
hippy Maureen, after his marriage breaks up and&nbsp;
his daughter starts her first year at NYU film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:56.820
school. A retrospective of Harold's work reunites&nbsp;
Danny with his siblings, financial planner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.820 --> 00:10:01.980
Matthew and Xerox employee Jean. But when their&nbsp;
father suffers a health scare, the emotionally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.980 --> 00:10:06.900
estranged children are suddenly confronted with&nbsp;
the various ways Harold has stunted their growth.

00:10:06.900 --> 00:10:11.580
"The Meyerowitz Stories" earned raves from&nbsp;
critics, particularly for its rich ensemble,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.580 --> 00:10:15.180
with Odie Henderson of RogerEbert.com writing -

00:10:15.180 --> 00:10:18.420
"Sandler finds the perfect line&nbsp;
between tragedy and comedy for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:22.140
Danny. He still does his hollering&nbsp;
angry man-child shtick, but here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.140 --> 00:10:26.460
it stems organically from the character&nbsp;
and is never used as a comedic crutch."

